window.docContents[133].resolve({"/References/#References":{"contents":"\n\nThe text in this chapter — especially the terse version —\ndoes not do a great job, early on, of connecting the concepts to\nthings that students will already know about. This makes the later\nparts unnecessarily puzzling.  (Also, the terse slides have way too\nmuch text and not NEARLY enough examples, quizzes, etc.)\nSampsa:\nThe exercise factorial_ref is tricky,\nbecause the definition of tseq is unhygienic\nwith respect to the variable x.\nThus, the following does not work unless the variable x is renamed.fact = λx : Nat , (λr : Ref (Nat → Nat) ,\n  r := λx : Nat , if x == 0 then 1 else x * !r (pred x);\n  !r x) (ref (λx : Nat , 0))\nFor the TERSE version, there are a bunch of slides in the\ntwo 2006 Chapter 13 lectures that would be great to include\nhere.The UPenn 2011 final exam has some good simple questions that\nshould be repeated here (question 5).More of the exercises should be marked recommended.\n\n\n\nMost real languages include impure features\n(\"computational effects\")...* mutable pointer structures* non-local control constructs (exceptions, continuations, etc.)* inter-process synchronization and communication* etc.Goal for this chapter: Formalize pointers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems","header":"9. References: Typing Mutable References","id":"/References/#References"}});